  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The cystic duct is usually 2 to 4 cm long. It may join the bile duct low; even then it rarely passes behind the duodenum, so a duct seen there is more likely the bile duct.",
      "refs": [
        "jmas-anatomy-cholecystectomy"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A low medial insertion of the cystic duct can be misread on imaging: its stones may be taken for distal bile duct stones, and at ERCP it may mimic the bile duct.",
      "refs": [
        "jmas-vasculobiliary-anatomy"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A long, parallel or spiral cystic duct makes its insertion hard to find at cholecystectomy, which risks injury to the bile duct.",
      "refs": [
        "jmas-vasculobiliary-anatomy"
      ]
    }
  ],
